{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And if it be too little, etc.</b> The verse is consistent both according to the view of Rabbi Yehudah and according to the view of Rabbi Yosi, who disagreed in Torat Kohanim (Mekhilta; Pesachim 99a). According to Rabbi Yehudah, who said that one member of the first company must remain [i.e., there must be leftovers from the first company], it is precise in its saying <b>if the household be too little</b> — meaning that the deficiency is from the company, not the entire company. And according to Rabbi Yosi, who said that they must not leave the Paschal [lamb] as it is, it is precise in its saying <b>from being from a lamb</b> — for a small amount of lamb will be diminished from the counted members, and upon this is the stringency that it not remain without those counted for it, and the verse is evenly balanced [between both views]." "<b>Further,</b> it appears that it intends to say that it is not the way of a lamb to be insufficient for its eaters, for an olive's-bulk of meat will be found in it even for the many who will be in the company." "<b>Or</b> it may intend that the blessing of holiness is abundant, and the required measure for those counted for it will be found in it, and the deficiency will be found only in the household — meaning the members of the company who would not suffice to eat it." "<b>According to the eating, you shall count.</b> This is explained according to the statement of the Sages, of blessed memory (Pesachim 88b), that a man slaughters on behalf of his minor son and daughter and also on behalf of his wife; they also said (Pesachim 87b): if she is accustomed to come to her father's [house], she eats from her father's, because she is habituated to eat there. And this is the meaning of <b>each man according to his eating</b> — meaning, concerning those who are accustomed to come to him to eat and rely upon his table, according to whatever he is counted with them, there they acquire their place." "<b>Further,</b> it may be explained according to their statement (Pesachim 90a) that an onein [one in the stage of pre-burial mourning] may slaughter on its behalf, even though at the time of slaughter he is unfit, since he is able to eat it at night they slaughter on his behalf. And this is the meaning of <b>according to his eating</b> — meaning, they proceed and are counted at the time of slaughter based on the time of eating." "<b>Further</b>, it may intend <b>according to his eating</b>, in accordance with what we learned in Pesaḥim (Pesaḥim 91a): a zav and so forth — they slaughter on his behalf, and we also say there that they slaughter and sprinkle the blood on behalf of one who immersed that same day (ṭevul yom) and one who lacks atonement (meḥusar kippurim), and Ulla said even on behalf of one impure through a sheretz, up to this point. And the reason is: anyone who is able to eat his paschal offering at the time of its eating — and that is the meaning of <b>according to his eating</b>; that is, they go only according to the eating of the paschal offering, and anyone who at the time of its eating is fit to eat it, even though at the time of slaughter he was not fit, they slaughter on his behalf. And according to this, the same ruling applies to one impure through corpse impurity if the seventh day of his purification fell on the fourteenth of Nisan: they slaughter on his behalf and he eats in the evening." "<b>And</b> what they said there in Torat Kohanim, cited in Pesaḥim fol. 90a — the reasoning of the Tanna who holds that ״וְלֹא יָכְלוּ לַעֲשׂוֹת הַפֶּסַח בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא״ (Numbers 9:6) means it was the seventh day of their impurity, and even so they were deferred to the second Pesaḥ — that applies specifically to corpse impurity, which is more stringent, and therefore it says (ibid.) ״לְנֶפֶשׁ אָדָם״, even though by evening they would have been pure. But according to Rabbi Yosi the Galilean (ibid.) we may say that even one impure through corpse impurity whose seventh day falls on the fourteenth of Nisan — they slaughter on his behalf. All the more so if he immersed, according to the view of the Tosafot in Pesaḥim fol. 90, and according to the view of the Raavad who objected to the Rambam in chapter 6 of Hilkhot Korban Pesaḥ — one should examine his words there, and it is the closest to the plain sense of the sugya."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:27:04.836862595Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}